  <ferc:NameOfReceiverOrTrusteeHoldingPropertyOfTheRespondent id="f-22" contextRef="c-1">N/A</ferc:NameOfReceiverOrTrusteeHoldingPropertyOfTheRespondent>
  <ferc:DateReceiverTookPossessionOfRespondentProperty id="f-23" contextRef="c-1" xsi:nil="true"></ferc:DateReceiverTookPossessionOfRespondentProperty>
  <ferc:GeneralInformationAboutStatesInWhichRespondentServicesWereRendered id="f-24" contextRef="c-1">Gas Transmission - Minnesota, Gas Transmission - Wisconsin, Gas Transmission - North Dakota</ferc:GeneralInformationAboutStatesInWhichRespondentServicesWereRendered>
  <ferc:GeneralInformationChangeInAuditorFlag id="f-25" contextRef="c-1">false</ferc:GeneralInformationChangeInAuditorFlag>
  <ferc:EngagementDateOfIndependentAuditor id="f-26" contextRef="c-1" xsi:nil="true"></ferc:EngagementDateOfIndependentAuditor>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-27" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;1.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;SUMMARY OF SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Organization and Nature of Operations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Viking Gas Transmission Company (Viking) owns a bi-directional 674-mile interstate natural gas pipeline extending from the U.S.-Canadian border near Emerson, Manitoba, to Marshfield, Wisconsin. Viking connects to other major pipeline systems, including TC Energy&#8217;s Canadian Mainline, Northern Natural Gas Company, Great Lakes Gas Transmission Company and ANR Pipeline Company, to provide service to markets in Minnesota, Wisconsin and North Dakota. &#8220;We&#8221;, &#8220;our&#8221; or &#8220;us&#8221; refer to Viking.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On December 31, 2024, ONEOK, Inc. (ONEOK), the former parent company of Viking, completed the sale of Viking to DT Midstream, Inc. (DTM). The sale also included two other interstate natural gas pipeline affiliates owned by ONEOK. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-left:36pt;padding-right:18pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;DT Midstream, through its subsidiaries, owns 100% of Viking. DTM does not guarantee any of our debts, liabilities or obligations. &#8220;Parent&#8221; refers to DTM. In 2025 and 2024, our parent, DTM, and former parent, ONEOK, respectively,through a services agreement (Services Agreement), provided to us similar services that they provided to their other affiliates and operated our assets according to our pipeline&#8217;s operating agreement. Both DTM and ONEOK provided a variety of services to us under the Services Agreement, including cash management and financial services, employee benefits provided through their respective benefit plans, legal and administrative services, insurance and office space. Where costs were specifically incurred on behalf of us, the costs were billed directly to us by DTM and ONEOK. In other situations, the costs were allocated to us through a variety of methods, depending upon the nature of the expense and activities. For example, a service that applies equally to all employees was allocated based upon the number of employees; however, an expense benefiting us, but which had no direct basis for allocation, was allocated by the modified Distrigas method, a widely recognized method of allocating costs, which uses a combination of ratios that includes gross plant and investment, operating income and payroll expense.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:2pt;padding-right:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;We hold a certificate of public convenience and necessity from the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) that authorizes us to construct and operate our pipeline system, which includes performing routine construction/abandonment activities and providing transportation service for our customers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Government Regulation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - The accompanying regulatory-basis financial statements are presented in accordance with the accounting requirements of the FERC as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America (GAAP). The accounting requirements of the FERC are subject to review by the FERC staff. Because many types of transactions are susceptible to varying interpretations under the Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, the amounts and classifications reported in the accompanying regulatory-basis financial statements may be subject to change at a later date upon final determination by the FERC staff.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - The preparation of our financial statements and related disclosures in accordance with the accounting requirements of the FERC requires us to make estimates and assumptions with respect to values or conditions that cannot be known with certainty that affect the reported amounts on our financial statements. Items that may be estimated include, but are not limited to, the economic useful lives of assets, expenses for services received but for which no invoice has been received, provision for income taxes, including any deferred tax valuation allowances, the results of litigation and various other recorded or disclosed amounts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;We evaluate these estimates on an ongoing basis using historical experience, consultation with experts and other methods we consider reasonable based on the particular circumstances. Nevertheless, actual results may differ significantly from the estimates. Any effects on our financial position or results of operations from revisions to these estimates are recorded in the period when the facts that give rise to the revision become known.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Revenue Recognition &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Transportation revenues are collected pursuant to a FERC tariff through transportation service agreements. The tariff specifies the general terms and conditions of transportation service on our natural gas pipeline system. Revenues are recognized as services are provided to our customers in an amount that reflects the consideration we expect to be entitled to receive in exchange for those services. Our payment terms vary by contract type, including requirements to issue payment before services are delivered. However, the term between customer prepayments, completion of our performance obligations, invoicing and receipt of payment due is not significant.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Firm service transportation contracts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - We reserve a stated transportation capacity and transport natural gas for our customers. The capacity reservation and transportation services are considered a bundled service, as we integrate them into one stand-ready obligation provided on a daily basis over the life of the agreement and satisfied over time. Fixed capacity reservation fees are allocated and evenly recognized in revenue. Capacity reservation fees that vary based on a stated or implied economic index and correspond with the costs to provide our services are recognized in revenue based on a daily effective fee rate. If the capacity reservation fees vary solely as a contract feature, contract assets or liabilities are recorded for the difference between the amount recorded in revenue and the amount billed to the customer. Transportation fees are recognized in revenue as those services are provided and are dependent on the volume transported by our customer, which is at our customer&#8217;s discretion. We use the output method based on the passage of time to measure satisfaction of the performance obligation associated with our daily stand-ready services. Our firm service agreements extend for various terms with termination dates through March 2036.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Interruptible transportation contracts - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;We agree to transport natural gas on our pipelines between the customer&#8217;s specified nominated-receipt and delivery points if capacity is available after satisfying firm transportation service obligations. The transaction price is based on the transportation fees times the volumes transported. We use the output method based on delivery of product to the customer to measure satisfaction of the performance obligation. The total consideration for delivered volumes is recorded in revenue at the time of delivery, when the customer obtains control. We have interruptible transportation service agreements and other transportation service agreements with numerous shippers. Revenues from interruptible transportation contracts were $1.0 million in 2025 and $1.7 million in 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Park-and-loan service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - A service offered to customers where we agree to receive (park) or deliver (loan) natural gas at a nominated point for delivery to or receipt from the customer at the same point at a specified later date. Customers reserve the right to park or loan natural gas based on a specified quantity when capacity is available on the pipeline. Revenues from park-and-loan services were immaterial in 2025 and 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Under the capacity release provisions of our tariff, shippers under firm contracts are allowed to release all or part of their capacity either permanently for the full term of the contract or temporarily. A temporary capacity release does not relieve the original contract shipper from its payment obligations if the replacement shipper fails to pay for the capacity temporarily released to it.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 4 for our revenue disclosures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Operations and Maintenance - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Operations and maintenance primarily includes (i) payroll and benefit costs, (ii) third-party costs for operations, maintenance and integrity management, regulatory compliance and environmental safety, and (iii) other business-related service costs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Customer Accounts Receivable &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- Customer accounts receivable represent valid claims against nonaffiliated customers for services rendered. We assess the creditworthiness of our counterparties on an ongoing basis and require security, including prepayments and other forms of collateral, when appropriate. Outstanding customer receivables are reviewed regularly for possible nonpayment indicators, and allowances for credit losses are recorded based upon management&#8217;s estimate of collectability, current conditions and supportable forecasts at each balance sheet date. We have not recorded allowances for credit losses as of December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Inventory -&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; Account 154 &#8220;Plant materials and operating supplies&#8221; are valued at average cost. Account 117.4 "Gas owed to system gas", which represents the net position of commodity imbalances, is valued at the current market price. Noncurrent natural gas is classified as property and valued at cost. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Commodity Imbalances &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- Imbalances occur when the actual amount of natural gas delivered from or received by a pipeline system differs from the contractual amount of natural gas to be delivered or received. These imbalances can be settled through cash outs (purchases or sales at the current month index price) or in-kind (with natural gas at some time in the future), subject to the terms of our tariff agreement. Commodity imbalances can include imbalances with associated companies. We account for our fuel cost, which represents the difference between gas retained from our customers and the gas consumed in operations, through a tracking mechanism and record it as an imbalance. Our fuel costs are expected to be recovered or refunded in subsequent periods. We value these imbalances due to or from shippers and operators each month at the current market price.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#3051f2;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Imbalances due from others are reported on our Balance Sheets in Account 174 &#8220;Miscellaneous current and accrued assets.&#8221; Imbalances owed to others are reported on our Balance Sheets in Account 242 &#8220;Miscellaneous current and accrued liabilities.&#8221;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Our properties are stated at cost, including allowance for funds used during construction (AFUDC). Generally, the cost of property retired or sold, plus removal costs, less salvage, is charged to accumulated depreciation. Gains and losses from sales or transfers of an entire operating unit or system of our properties are recognized in income. Maintenance and repairs are charged directly to expense.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The debt portion of AFUDC represents the cost of borrowed funds used to finance construction activities. We capitalize interest costs during the construction or upgrade of qualifying assets. The debt portion of AFUDC is recorded as a reduction to interest expense. The equity portion of AFUDC represents the capitalization of the estimated average cost of equity used during the construction of major projects and is recorded in the cost of our properties and as a credit to the allowance for equity funds used during construction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Our properties are depreciated using the straight-line method over their estimated useful lives. Generally, we apply depreciation rates to functional groups of property having similar economic lives.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#3051f2;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;We periodically conduct depreciation studies to assess the economic lives of our assets. These depreciation studies are completed as a part of our rate proceedings or tariff filings, and the changes in economic lives, if applicable, are implemented prospectively as of the approved effective date. Changes in the estimated economic lives of our property, plant and equipment could have a material effect on our financial position or results of operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The composite depreciation rate, including negative salvage, for transmission plant ranged from 1.9% to 7.2% for the years ended December 31, 2025 and 1.9% and 7.1% for year ended December 31, 2024. The rates for general plant range from 2.2% to 10.0% for the year ended December 31, 2025 and 2024. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;We collect estimated future removal costs related to our transmission facilities in our tariff rates, also known as negative salvage). Estimated costs associated with the future removal of transmission facilities are collected through depreciation as allowed by the FERC. These amounts do not represent asset retirement obligations as defined by the Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB) Accounting Standards Codification (ASC) 410, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Accounting for Asset Retirement Obligations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, plant and equipment on our Balance Sheets include construction work in process for capital projects that have not yet been placed in service and, therefore, are not being depreciated. Assets are transferred out of construction work in process when they are substantially complete and ready for their intended use.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Additions to property, plant and equipment included in accounts payable were $0.5 million and $2.6 million at December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively, which are not included in capital expenditures on our Statements of Cash Flows.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- We assess our long-lived assets for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate that an asset&#8217;s carrying amount may not be recoverable. An impairment is indicated if the carrying amount of a long-lived asset exceeds the sum of the undiscounted future cash flows expected to result from the use and eventual disposition of the asset. If an impairment is indicated, we adjust the carrying value of the asset, if necessary and upon regulatory approval, to its estimated recoverable value. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In 2025, there were no events or changes in circumstances to indicate that our assets&#8217; carrying amounts may not be recoverable. We continue to experience stable earnings with all of our transportation capacity fully contracted with firm commitments, and have not experienced any indicators that this will change in the future. We determined that there were no asset impairments in 2025 and 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Our impairment tests require the use of assumptions and estimates such as industry economic factors and the profitability of future business strategies. If actual results are not consistent with our assumptions and estimates or our assumptions and estimates change due to new information, we may be exposed to future impairment charges.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulation &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;During the rate-making process, regulatory authorities set the framework for what we can charge customers for our services and establish the manner that our costs are accounted for, including allowing us to defer recognition of certain costs and permitting recovery of the amounts through rates over time as opposed to expensing such costs as incurred. Certain examples of types of regulatory guidance include costs for fuel and losses, acquisition costs, contributions in aid of construction, charges for depreciation, and gains or losses on disposition of assets. This allows us to stabilize rates over time rather than passing such costs on to the customer for immediate recovery. Actions by regulatory authorities could have an effect on the amount we may charge our customers. Any difference in the amount recoverable and the amount deferred is recorded as income or expense at the time of the regulatory action.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;At December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, we recorded regulatory assets of $5.0 million and $1.6 million respectively. Our regulatory assets are included in Account 182.3 &#8220;Other regulatory assets" and Account 186 "Miscellaneous deferred debits" on our Balance Sheets. Our regulatory assets are related to our Load Management Services tracker, our Electric Power Cost Recovery tracker, the deferred tax liability for the equity component of AFUDC, deferred regulatory commission and rate case expenses, which are being recovered or are expected to be recovered from our customers as a result of approved rate proceedings over varying time periods up to 45 years. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;At December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, we recorded regulatory liabilities of $8.8 million and $10.0 million in Account 254 &#8220;Other regulatory liabilities,&#8221; respectively. Regulatory liabilities include cash out penalties, which are distributed to non-offending customers and a regulatory liability arising from the remeasurement of deferred tax liabilities as a result of the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Due to the extensive changes to U.S. tax laws and corporate tax rates with the passage of the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017, we revalued our existing accumulated deferred income tax (ADIT) liabilities in Account 283 &#8220;Accumulated deferred income taxes - other.&#8221; This revaluation resulted in an $8.9 million adjustment (before gross-up)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#3051f2;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;to our ADIT, which was charged through Account 254 &#8220;Other regulatory liabilities&#8221; in the fourth quarter 2017. All of the excess ADIT relates to utility plant and is protected and will be included as a component of rate base until the excess is fully amortized. For the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024, $0.5 million of the balance in Account 254 &#8220;Other regulatory liabilities&#8221; has been amortized to Account 411.1 &#8220;Provision for deferred income taxes - Credit, utility operating income&#8221; through a normalization method of accounting. These amounts include the tax gross-up on the regulatory liability. The remaining balance will be amortized over the life of the underlying assets that created the reserve, which is approximately 23 years. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - For the year ended December 31, 2025, we were included on the consolidated United States federal income tax return of DT Midstream. For the year ended December 31, 2024, we were included in the consolidated tax return of our former parent, ONEOK. However, the FERC establishes the method of accounting for income taxes and we report income tax related amounts as if we were filing a stand alone return. In our Balance Sheets, our income tax liabilities have been calculated as if we were filing a separate return.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Deferred income taxes are provided for the difference between the financial statement and income tax basis of assets and liabilities and carryforward items based on income tax laws and rates existing at the time the temporary differences are expected to reverse if we were a taxpayer. The effect on deferred tax assets and liabilities of a change in tax rates is recorded as regulatory assets and regulatory liabilities in the period that includes the enactment date.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;We account for uncertainties in income taxes using a benefit recognition model with a two-step approach: a more-likely-than-not recognition criterion, and a measurement attribute that measures the position as the largest amount of tax benefit that is greater than a 50% likelihood of being realized upon ultimate settlement. If the benefit does not meet the more likely than not criteria for being sustained on its technical merits, no benefit will be recorded. Uncertain tax positions that relate only to timing of when an item is included on a tax return are considered to have met the recognition threshold. We reflect penalties and interest as they become applicable for tax provisions that do not meet the more-likely-than-not recognition threshold and measurement attribute. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Retirement and Other Postretirement Employee Benefits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &#8211; DT Midstream sponsors defined contribution retirement savings plans, and participation in one of these plans is available to substantially all employees. Employee contributions are matched up to certain predefined and Internal Revenue Service limits based on eligible compensation and each employee's contribution rate, and contribute additional amounts in lieu of traditional pension and post-employment healthcare benefits. Costs recognized for these plans were $1.2 million for 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Prior to the acquisition on December 31, 2024, our former parent company, ONEOK, maintained a defined benefit pension plan covering certain employees and former employees, which closed to new participants in 2005. In addition, ONEOK maintained a supplemental executive retirement plan for the benefit of certain officers who participate in its defined benefit pension plan. All ONEOK employees were eligible to make salary deferrals and receive company matching contributions under ONEOK&#8217;s 401(k) Plan, and employees that did not participate in the defined benefit pension plan were also eligible to receive quarterly and annual profit sharing contributions under ONEOK&#8217;s 401(k) Plan. ONEOK sponsored welfare plans that provided postretirement medical and life insurance benefits to certain employees hired prior to 2017 who retired with at least five years of service. ONEOK was responsible for benefits accrued under its plans and allocated certain costs based on payroll charged to its affiliates. Costs recognized for these plans were $1.2 million for 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Fair Value Measurements &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Our financial instruments consist of accounts receivable and accounts payable. The carrying amounts of the financial instruments approximate fair value due to the short-term nature of these items. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Asset retirement obligations represent legal obligations associated with the retirement of long-lived assets that result from the acquisition, construction, development and/or normal use of the asset. We recognize the fair value of a liability for an asset retirement obligation in the period when it is incurred if a reasonable estimate of the fair value can be made. We are not able to estimate reasonably the fair value of the asset retirement obligations for our pipeline assets, because the settlement dates are indeterminable given our expected continued use of the assets with proper maintenance. We expect our pipeline assets, for which we are unable to estimate reasonably the fair value of the asset retirement obligation, will continue in operation as long as supply and demand for natural gas exists. Based on the widespread use of natural gas for heating and cooking activities for residential users and electric-power generation for commercial users, we expect supply and demand to exist for the foreseeable future.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Contingencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; -&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Our accounting for contingencies covers a variety of business activities, including contingencies for legal and environmental exposures. We accrue these contingencies when our assessments indicate that it is probable that a liability has been incurred or an asset will not be recovered and an amount can be estimated reasonably. We expense legal fees as incurred and base our legal liability estimates on currently available facts and our estimates of the ultimate outcome or resolution. Accruals for estimated losses from environmental remediation obligations generally are recognized no later than completion of a remediation feasibility study. Recoveries of environmental remediation costs from other parties are recorded as assets when their receipt is deemed probable. Our expenditures for environmental evaluation, mitigation, remediation and compliance to date have not been significant in relation to our financial position or results of operations, and our expenditures related to environmental matters had no significant effect on earnings or cash flows during 2025 and 2024. Actual results may differ from our estimates resulting in an impact, positive or negative, on earnings. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 5 for additional discussion of contingencies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Presentation of Cash Flows Associated with Cash Management Program &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- During 2025, while DTM was our parent, DTM provided us with cash management services, which allowed us the ability to sweep cash to or from DTM. DTM used the cash management program to settle intercompany transactions between participating affiliates. An associated company payable resulted when cash was swept to us from DTM, while an associated company receivable resulted when cash was swept from us to DTM. Associated company payables and receivables were recorded and deemed settled to a cash account. Our policy is to present the net cash sweep activity in financing activities when the associated company balance to DTM was in a payable position, and as investing activities when the associated company balance from DTM was in a receivable position.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;During 2024, while ONEOK was our parent, ONEOK provided us with cash management services, which allowed us the ability to sweep cash to or from ONEOK. ONEOK used the cash management program to settle intercompany transactions between participating affiliates. An associated company payable resulted when cash was swept to us from ONEOK, while an associated company receivable resulted when cash was swept from us to ONEOK. Associated company payables and receivables were recorded and deemed settled to a cash account. Our policy is to present the net cash sweep activity in financing activities when the associated company balance to ONEOK was in a payable position, and as investing activities when the associated company balance from ONEOK was in a receivable position.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Presentation of Cash Flows Related to Contributions and Distributions with Associated Companies &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Contributions from and distributions to DTM and ONEOK, while they were our parent, were deemed cash settled through our accounts with associated companies and were recorded between us and DTM or ONEOK. Our policy is to present these contributions and distributions gross in the Statements of Cash Flows as sources or uses of cash flows from financing activities, as appropriate. In 2025, we recorded distributions by us to DTM of $96 million. In 2024, we received capital contributions from ONEOK of $113.7 million. Proceeds were used by Viking to repay long term debt, repay short term debt under the cash management program and fund capital expenditures. We recorded distributions by us to ONEOK of $8.0 million in 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Debt &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; Associated Companies &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- In June 2025, we entered into a long-term promissory note with DT Midstream Inc. for a revolving line of credit with a maximum principal amount of $300 million, maturing December 2029. Account 223 &#8220;Advances from associated companies&#8221; had a balance of $84 million as of December 31, 2025 and no balance as of December 31, 2024. Interest on long-term debt to DT Midstream is calculated annually at an interest rate of 5.895%. Total interest paid on the long-term debt to DT Midstream was $2.3 million for the year ended December 31, 2025 and no interest paid on debt from associated companies in the year ended December 31, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Subsequent Event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;s - We have evaluated subsequent events through &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ff0000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;April&#160;17, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;, the date our financial statements were available, and we believe all required subsequent events disclosures have been made.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Recently Issued Accounting Standards Update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Changes to GAAP are established by the FASB in the form of accounting standards updates (ASUs) to the FASB Accounting Standards Codification. We consider the applicability and impact of all ASUs. ASUs not discussed herein were assessed and determined to be either not applicable or clarifications of ASUs previously issued. Except as discussed below, there have been no new accounting pronouncements that have become effective or have been issued that are of significance or potential significance to us.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In December 2023, the FASB issued ASU 2023-09, Income Taxes (Topic 740): Improvements to Income Tax Disclosures. The amendments improve transparency of income tax disclosure requirements, primarily through enhanced disclosures of rate reconciliation and income taxes paid. The amendments are effective for annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2024. We early adopted this ASU effective for the year ended December 31,2025, and applied the disclosure requirements retrospectively to all prior periods presented.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;LONG-TERM DEBT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;At December 31, 2025 and 2024, we had no long-term debt outstanding other than the long-term promissory note with DT Midstream, Inc. See Note 1 for discussion of that promissory note. In December 2024, in accordance with the purchase and sale agreement, we paid off a $60 million senior unsecured loan in full prior to the sale to DTM. Total interest paid on the Term Loan for the year ended December 31, 2024 was $4.0 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;3.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;INCOME TAXES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The following table sets forth our provision for income taxes for the periods indicated:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:5pt"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:72.692%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:11.637%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.532%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:11.639%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Years Ended December 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(Thousands of dollars)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Current income tax expense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 7.75pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Federal &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2,819&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;756&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 7.75pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;State&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;893&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;154&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 12.25pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Total current income tax expense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;3,712&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;910&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:2pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Deferred income tax benefit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 7.75pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&l